            "text": "Based on an analysis of the provided metadata (titles and authors), the texts in the Corpus of Lake District Writing (CLDW) can be categorized into six primary genres. Because many works from this period blend forms (e.g., William Combe’s *Doctor Syntax* is a comic tour written in verse; Robert Southey’s *Letters from England* is a fictional epistolary travelogue), they have been grouped according to their primary structural format.\n\nHere is the genre assessment for the 80 texts in the corpus:\n\n### Genre Breakdown\n\n| Genre | Number of Texts | Percentage of Corpus |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: |\n| **Tours, Travelogues & Journals**\u003cbr\u003e*(e.g., rambles, journeys, excursions, itineraries)* | 42 | 52.50% |\n| **Guidebooks & Handbooks**\u003cbr\u003e*(e.g., tourist companions, climbing guides)* | 13 | 16.25% |\n| **Poetry**\u003cbr\u003e*(e.g., odes, sonnets, chorographical & comic poems)* | 8 | 10.00% |\n| **Topographies & Surveys**\u003cbr\u003e*(e.g., geographical descriptions, lithological observations)* | 7 | 8.75% |\n| **Essays, Letters & Sketches**\u003cbr\u003e*(e.g., literary studies, personal correspondence, fragments)* | 6 | 7.50% |\n| **Fiction & Drama**\u003cbr\u003e*(e.g., novels, tales, comic opera)* | 4 | 5.00% |\n| **TOTAL** | **80** | **100.00%** |\n\n***Note:** Some late-published works (e.g., Gell, Ruskin, Wilberforce) are dated in the metadata by their modern publication year but reflect historical tours/poems.*\n\n### Commentary on Genre Balance\n\nThe corpus shows a **highly uneven and heavily skewed genre distribution**. \n\nIt is overwhelmingly dominated by non-fiction travel and tourism writing. **Tours, Travelogues & Journals** alone account for more than half of the entire corpus (52.5%). When combined with **Guidebooks & Handbooks** (16.25%) and **Topographies** (8.75%), nearly 80% of the texts are fundamentally dedicated to describing the landscape for travelers, scientists, and armchair tourists. \n\nThis imbalance is not a flaw in the corpus, but rather an accurate reflection of the historical reality of Lake District literature. Writing about this region was largely catalyzed by the \"Picturesque\" movement of the late 18th century and the subsequent explosion of Victorian domestic tourism. \n\nInterestingly, despite the Lake District being globally famous for its association with the Romantic poets (like Wordsworth, Coleridge, and Southey), purely literary forms—such as **Poetry** and **Fiction/Drama**—make up a distinct minority of the corpus (just 15% combined). Ultimately, the CLDW functions primarily as a corpus of topographical, observational, and travel-oriented prose.",
